plan Transactions
title   Transactions
status  archived
goal
    Commit actor-owned mutations with exact concurrency, isolated previews, and truthful remote
    uncertainty. Preserve canonical data and current projection when older attempts finish late.
    This proposed Plan is pending approval. It establishes no shipped implementation claim.
outcomes
    O1  Exact transaction families preserve input, key, and failure types
        The exact transaction API below fixes descriptor authoring, exact family methods, closed
        state union, field presence, and explicit writes/outcomes. Preserve P/K/A/E/R through
        descriptor, named actor O, machine, app, Runtime, fixture, and Story. Omitted key projector
        means K = []. Descriptor/K identity uses @plan.Resources.O1 and its inline canonical-key
        rules. Runtime requirements remain inferred R.

        transaction(config) constructs an inert descriptor with these accepted authoring fields:

        | Field | Required | Accepted relationship |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | id | Yes | Exact authored descriptor ID |
        | key | No | (params:P) => K; omission gives K=[] |
        | commit | Yes | (params:P, { signal }) => Effect.Effect<A,E,R> |
        | persist | No | boolean; omitted means false |
        | concurrency | No | reject / cancel / allow / serialize; omission remains @Q2 |

        commit receives the supported AbortSignal and preserves exact A/E/R. Its result is not a
        Promise or an implicit canonical resource write. @Q2 leaves only the omitted concurrency
        policy unsettled; no adapter reporting callback or identity getter is added.

        Key and getState are passive; a missing exact read synthesizes idle without allocating,
        acquiring, refreshing, mutating, or starting work. commit(P,options?) constructs an inert
        finite plan. Only returned accepted event actions under @plan.Statecharts.O3 admit it.
        Activation, reads, completion, reconciliation, memory/store revision, and reentry never
        admit/readmit a finite commit. Retry requires a new explicit event and commit.

        Public state distinguishes idle, pending, success, failure, defect, interrupted, and unknown
        with reconcileRequired true. It contains no raw Cause or historical-attempt collection.
        Binding introduces no parent machine/memory/event/selector/route/preview/invalidation type
        graph. The family has no getData, retry, scope, lane, registry, or historical-attempt 
        method.

        TransactionFamily<P, K extends unknown[], A, E> has exactly these methods:

        | Method | Exact signature |
        | --- | --- |
        | key | (params: P): K |
        | getState | (key: K): TransactionState<A,E,K> |
        | commit | (params: P, options?: CommitOptions<P,A,E>): TransactionCommitPlan |
        | cancel | (key: K): CancellationPlan |

        TransactionState<A,E,K> is the exact closed union below. Every lane has key:K. Fields absent
        from a lane remain absent.

        | status | Other exact fields |
        | --- | --- |
        | idle | No generation. |
        | pending | generation:number. |
        | success | generation:number; value:A. |
        | failure | generation:number; error:E. |
        | defect | generation:number; defect:unknown. |
        | interrupted | generation:number. |
        | unknown | generation:number; reconcileRequired:true. |

        Generation is absent in idle and present in all admitted attempt lanes. Unknown is distinct
        from interruption. Absent fields remain absent. Counts and generations are safe non-negative
        integers; admitted occurrence ordinals and generations begin at one.

        Each authored outcome mapper returns one exact event envelope, never its constructor
        Result. The failure mapper is unavailable when E=never. Mappers receive no Exit, Cause,
        state, or lifecycle. Complete classification belongs to @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2.

        CommitOptions uses the caller-visible object fields writes and outcomes. The optional
        writes callback has the form writes: ({ value }) => [O.resource.setData(K,data), ...].
        Its argument is { value:A }; its result is an array of explicit resource setData plans.
        The optional outcomes object contains authored mappings with these exact names:

        | outcomes member | Mapper argument |
        | --- | --- |
        | success | value:A |
        | failure | error:E |
        | defect | No argument. |
        | interrupt | No argument. |

        Callers use commit(P,{ writes, outcomes }); either option may be omitted. No completion-side
        invalidates or clears option exists. The [examples](references/README.md)
        illustrate these normative option containers without adding support-alias representations.

        `persist` defaults false. True opts in actor-owned durable facts only when the owning stable
        actor is independently persistable. Adapter values never become canonical resource data
        implicitly. The named option and plan aliases describe semantic roles and add no public
        exports or methods.

    O2  Actor-local exact-key concurrency settles and fences each occurrence
        Attempts are actor-local per exact descriptor/K. Completion authority requires the current
        actor incarnation, descriptor/K, operation kind, occurrence ordinal, and applicable
        generation. Shared canonical writes additionally require current store-generation authority.
        Match fences immediately before publication; requested fiber interruption alone is
        insufficient. Every admitted occurrence settles once. Late success/failure/finalizer facts
        can settle bounded evidence of their own occurrence but cannot affect replacement actors,
        reused refs, collected entries, current projection, or routed events.

        The concurrency policies are exact:

        | Policy | Admission | Completion authority |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | reject | Decline second active attempt; no occurrence. | Current attempt. |
        | cancel | Cancel prior, then admit replacement. | Replacement. |
        | allow | Admit independent generations. | Current generation for projection/route. |
        | serialize | Queue exact-key materialized P FIFO. | Dequeued occurrence. |

        Reject consumes no ordinal. Cancel settles the prior attempt, removes its preview, and gives
        replacement a fresh ordinal. Allow keeps ordered preview layers. Serialize applies preview
        only at dequeue; canceled queued work settles without starting its adapter. Registration of
        cancellation and fencing precedes any possible adapter execution. Concurrency for another
        actor or another exact key remains independent.

        Accepted cancel(K) affects current finite occurrences owned by that actor for K. Planned
        cancellation/release/disposal records cleanup without an authored interruption mapper.
        Ordinary operation interruption retains its distinct classified outcome. Finalizers release
        once in reverse acquisition order. Shared lifecycle/suspension/disposal rules belong to
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5; cleanup cannot roll back an already published transition.

        Attempts have one-based monotonic non-reused ordinals. Actor incarnation and admission
        counters follow @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5. Reserve
        safe-integer headroom for terminal publication/cleanup before admission or restore;
        exhaustion fails without adapter start, wrap, reuse, or partial mutation.

        The current stabilized binding determines ordinary transaction projection. Removing,
        replacing, suspending, disposing, or reentering a binding never creates another attempt.
        Retain bounded terminal facts and cursors; old attempts belong to bounded inspection
        evidence under @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O3. No actor-lifetime terminal registry exists.
    O3  Previews stay actor-local and canonical settlement remains atomic
        An optimistic layer retains its initiating actor incarnation, transaction occurrence,
        descriptor, and K. Only that actor's effective resource reads apply its ordered layers.
        Other actors and unbound/updater reads use canonical base under @plan.Resources.O2.
        Applying, removing, or replaying a layer changes only its actor's projection cursor. It is
        never shared canonical truth.

        Success promotes the accepted explicit canonical write atomically before mapped outcomes.
        Use the captured base revision as compare-and-set authority. If base changed, preserve
        current remote canonical truth and record an initiating-actor conflict issue. Never let
        stale local promotion overwrite the current base. Failure or defect removes only its
        layer and replays remaining layers in order. Interruption of never-invoked work does the 
        same.
        Cancellation after adapter invocation retains unknown/reconciliation truth when removing
        local provisional state, as @O4 specifies. Other actors receive
        only canonical facts and never another actor's preview/rollback.

        Commit may declare authoritative setData writes as specified under @O1. Transaction values
        never become canonical implicitly. Updaters read committed base and undefined declines;
        accepted writes fence old lookups and publish under @plan.Resources.O4. Whole
        action/completion batches validate before any publication under @plan.Statecharts.O3.
        Invalid prefixes publish nothing. An equal canonical value still settles its admitted
        occurrence once; continuing resource value notification follows @plan.Resources.O3.
    O4  Cancellation reports irreversible remote uncertainty honestly
        The application adapter owns the actual remote identity and its independent durable
        journal. Before potentially
        irreversible remote dispatch, it records remote identity/boundary state and awaits durable
        acknowledgment. A failed or unacknowledged journal record prevents dispatch. Adapter
        invocation may begin this preparation; invocation is distinct from remote dispatch.

        Flow conservatively treats an invoked adapter as possibly dispatched. Cancellation after
        invocation stops local observation, fences late publication, and projects unknown with
        reconcileRequired true. Queued work whose adapter never invoked may settle interrupted.
        Flow does not verify the exact physical boundary mid-flight or claim remote rollback.
        Local preview removal cannot reverse a remote effect.

        The adapter journal is independent of optional Runtime Persistence. No-provider,
        persist-false, and local-owner transactions remain valid; they do not acquire implicit
        Runtime durability. A committed projection or pending/coalesced provider write is not
        journal acknowledgment. The accepted commit(params,{signal}) adapter form gains no method,
        reporting protocol, or public identity getter.

        Reconciliation reuses the same remote idempotency identity; it is not a new request or
        automatic retry. Compensation is a separate explicit operation with separate identity. No
        implicit outbox, saga, rollback API, or remote-effect reversal is introduced. Accepted
        remote canonical success remains truthful when local preview promotion conflicts.

        Remote-certainty projection is separate from complete Cause classification under
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2. Defect/typed-failure/interruption precedence and cleanup
        evidence remain intact. Flow preserves its own actor/descriptor/K/occurrence/generation and
        settlement evidence. It does not capture the adapter's private remote identity or journal,
        and it does not manage their recovery. The application adapter owns reconciliation of that
        same independently recorded remote identity.

        Persist defaults false. A durable transaction declaration retains only accepted actor-owned
        facts for an independently durable stable owner under @plan.PersistenceV2.O2. Hydration never
        replays pending commit work. Restored pending remote work remains unknown/reconcileRequired
        unless accepted evidence rules out adapter invocation, including invocation after capture.
        A saved not-yet-invoked fact alone cannot establish non-invocation after that cut.
        Reconciliation uses the adapter-recorded remote identity without a new request.
        Terminal state restores only in the accepted production representation under
        @plan.PersistenceV2.O4. K never reconstructs executable P.
decisions
    D1  Concurrency belongs to the actor and exact key
        Four policies define admission and current completion authority without a cross-actor lane.
        Queue/fiber topology is private. Observable ordering, one settlement, and fencing are
        binding.
    D2  Local preview and remote truth have different authority
        Preview layers support the initiating actor. Explicit fenced writes establish canonical
        data. Crossing the irreversible boundary preserves uncertainty until explicit
        reconciliation.
    D3  Remote journal identity remains application-owned
        The user selected adapter-owned actual remote identity, acknowledged journaling and
        reconciliation. @O4 keeps the fixed commit(params,{signal}) API. Flow reports conservative
        uncertainty and its own attempt evidence without a private identity capture or recovery
        promise. No new reporting callback or mandatory Runtime Persistence is introduced.

constraints
    C1  Keep the accepted finite mutation surface closed
        Add no transaction scope/lane, generic registry, descriptor-only keyed identity, retry
        helper, cross-actor scheduler, terminal-history registry, public occurrence handle, or raw
        Cause. Add no parent capture, implicit result promotion, completion invalidates/clears
        options, or partially expanded target mutation.
    C2  Durable operations do not imply durable actors or replay
        persist true never makes a local/opaque actor durable. Never serialize executable commit
        work, queue cells, callbacks, fibers, or Scopes. No cancellation path claims remote
        reversal.
verification
    V1  Exact API, state, and inference
        Establish @O1. Planned, unbuilt checks:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/transaction-types.test.ts` and
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/transaction.test.ts`. Compile exact
        P/K/A/E/R through all consumers, K=[] omission, state narrowing, and typed success/failure
        events. Reject wrong/missing/widened P or K, E=never failure mapper, forbidden
        methods/options, parent-type capture, and raw Cause access. Read absent identities and
        construct unreturned plans; pass with no allocation, revision, ordinal, or adapter start.

        Compile the exact commit(P,{ writes:({value})=>[O.resource.setData(K,data)], outcomes:{...} 
        })
        form. Infer A in writes.value and success, E in failure, and exact resource K/data in each
        write. Reject renamed option containers, wrong callback arguments, completion invalidates/
        clears, and a mapper returning Result<EventEnvelope,Diagnostic> instead of its success
        envelope. A validated event-constructor success must compile without an overload.
        Compile required id/commit, optional key/persist/concurrency, and exact adapter P/A/E/R.
        Reject unknown constructor fields and each invalid concurrency spelling. Omitted-policy
        behavior remains blocked by @Q2.
    V2  Concurrency, settlement, and lifetime races
        Establish @O2. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/transaction-concurrency.test.ts`. Run
        reject/cancel/allow/serialize for equal/different keys and two actors. Pass when reject
        consumes no ordinal, cancel replaces once, allow preserves layers while fencing current
        projection/route, and serialize starts FIFO with preview only on dequeue. Canceled queued
        work must never call the adapter. Every admitted occurrence settles once.

        Race completion/finalizer against cancel, replacement, suspension, disposal, new
        incarnation, collected entry, and exhausted counters. Pass with no stale canonical write,
        current projection, or mapped event; no planned interruption mapper; exactly-once release
        and terminal evidence; no partial admission at the safe-integer boundary.
    V3  Preview, explicit write, and compare-and-set proofs
        Establish @O3. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/transaction-preview.test.ts`. Stack multiple
        owner layers, compare another actor/unbound/updater view, then complete layers in different
        orders. Pass when rollback removes only its layer and replays the rest, other actors see
        canonical facts only, and success writes publish before mapped outcomes. Change canonical
        base during preview; pass when compare-and-set preserves remote truth and creates only
        initiating conflict evidence. Test updater decline, equal success, invalid mixed batch, and
        stale lookup completion; no implicit write or valid-prefix publication.
    V4  Remote boundary and durable reconciliation proofs
        Establish @O4. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/transaction-remote.test.ts`. Cancel queued
        work before adapter invocation; pass with no adapter start and interrupted settlement.
        Cancel invoked work before, during, and after physical remote dispatch; every case must
        retain unknown/reconcileRequired and fence late publication without claiming rollback.
        Reconciliation must reuse identity and compensation must use a distinct explicit operation.

        Hold and fail the adapter journal's durability acknowledgment independently of Flow
        publication. Observe adapter invocation separately from actual remote-request dispatch.
        Pass when no remote request starts while acknowledgment is pending or failed. A published
        snapshot or queued/coalesced provider write cannot substitute for journal acknowledgment.

        Cover no Persistence provider, persist-false transaction, and local/nonpersistent owner
        separately from an opted-in stable actor with a provider. Each remains admitted, and each
        remote adapter must satisfy its independent journal guard without enabling Runtime
        persistence implicitly. Re-read the adapter's acknowledged record across crash/cancel
        boundaries; explicit reconciliation must reuse its identity, never issue a new request.
        No Flow-managed journal recovery or exact mid-flight boundary-reporting API may be assumed.

        Restore pending local, remote-unknown, and terminal facts under @plan.PersistenceV2.V4.
        Include its saved-before-invocation cut followed by dispatch, delayed persistence and crash.
        Pass with conservative unknown unless evidence excludes later invocation, no commit replay
        or new remote request, no K-to-P inversion, and no implicit actor durability.
        Full Cause classification runs through @plan.DiagnosticsContract.V2;
        uncertainty must not erase defect precedence or cleanup/evidence diagnostics.
questions
    Q2  Which concurrency policy applies when the optional field is omitted?
        The four explicit policies and concurrency field optionality are accepted. Their sources
        do not specify the omitted-field policy. Do not infer a default from a named example or a
        legacy implementation.
